  "summary": "A Dutch movement reducing art and design to straight lines, right angles and primaries, seeking universal harmony.",
  "description": "Led by Theo van Doesburg and Piet Mondrian around the journal De Stijl, the movement stripped painting, architecture and furniture to horizontal and vertical elements in primary colors plus black and white. It pursued a spiritualized universal order — Mondrian called it Neo-Plasticism. Gerrit Rietveld's Red-Blue Chair and Schröder House are its clearest spatial statements. Its radical abstraction influenced the Bauhaus and mid-century graphic design.",
